# Image Assets

## Location

All block images live in **`assets/images/`**, never inside a block folder.

```
assets/images/
  home-hero-banner.png
  stores-map.png
  about-team-1.png
```

Block folders should contain only `.php`, `.scss`, `.css`, and optionally `.js` — no images.

## Figma Export Scale — Always 2x

When extracting raster images from Figma, **always export at 2x scale** so they render crisp on high-DPI displays. Never export at 1x.

- **Scale:** 2x (non-negotiable — never 1x)
- **Format:** PNG for photos and images with transparency; SVG for icons, logos, and vector art (scale doesn't apply to SVG)
- **Destination:** `assets/images/{descriptive-kebab-name}.png`

In markup, reference the 2x file directly — the browser handles DPR scaling via CSS sizing. Do **not** generate `@1x` / `@2x` pairs unless explicitly asked.

## Referencing in Markup

Use an **absolute path** from the site root (`/assets/images/...`), not a relative path:

```html
<!-- ✅ RIGHT -->
<img src="/assets/images/home-hero-banner.png" alt="Descriptive alt text">

<!-- ❌ WRONG — relative path -->
<img src="../../assets/images/home-hero-banner.png" alt="...">

<!-- ❌ WRONG — image inside block folder -->
<img src="/blocks/home-hero/banner.png" alt="...">
```

## Alt Text

- **Informative images** (photos, illustrations that carry meaning): descriptive `alt` text
- **Decorative images** (purely visual, no semantic value): `alt=""` — empty alt is required, not missing
- **Icons inside buttons:** the button's label handles accessibility; the icon can have `alt=""` or be an inline SVG with `aria-hidden="true"`

## Naming

Use descriptive, kebab-case names that indicate the block or purpose:

```
✅ stores-map.png
✅ about-team-headshot-1.png
✅ home-hero-banner.png
❌ img1.png
❌ Screenshot_2024-03-15.png
❌ Banner@2x.png
```

## Performance Attributes

Every `<img>` below the fold needs `loading="lazy" decoding="async"`. The above-the-fold LCP image (e.g. site-header logo, home-hero) stays eager — do **not** add `loading="lazy"` to it.

Add explicit `width` and `height` attributes whenever known, to prevent CLS as the image loads. Use the image's intrinsic pixel dimensions (a 96×29 logo gets `width="96" height="29"`); CSS still controls rendered size.

```html
<!-- ✅ Below-the-fold image -->
<img src="/assets/images/foo.png" alt="..." width="600" height="400" loading="lazy" decoding="async">

<!-- ✅ Above-the-fold LCP image — eager, no loading attribute -->
<img src="/assets/images/header-logo.svg" alt="Site name" width="120" height="32" decoding="async">

<!-- ❌ Below-the-fold image with no perf attributes -->
<img src="/assets/images/foo.png" alt="...">
```

See `scroll-performance.mdc` §6.

## Rendering ACF Images

ACF image fields store an attachment ID. Render them with `wp_get_attachment_image()` — never compose `<img>` markup by hand from the attachment URL. The helper auto-emits `srcset`, `sizes`, `width`, `height`, and the right loading hint.

```php
$image_id = get_field( 'image' );  // or $item['image'] in a repeater
if ( $image_id ) {
	echo wp_get_attachment_image(
		$image_id,
		'w768',
		false,
		[
			'class'   => 'img-cover',
			'loading' => 'lazy',
			'sizes'   => '(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px',
		]
	);
}
```

Pass `loading => 'lazy'` for below-the-fold; omit it for the LCP image.

### Custom Image Sizes

The theme registers four custom sizes in `functions/core/add-image-sizes.php`. Pick the smallest size that still renders crisp at the largest viewport the image hits:

| Size    | Use                                                          |
| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `w200`  | ACF preview thumbnails only (set in JSON `preview_size`)     |
| `h200`  | Square / aspect-locked thumbnails 200px tall                 |
| `w768`  | Default for in-content imagery (cards, repeater items)       |
| `w1400` | Wide imagery, hero-adjacent panels                           |
| `w1920` | Full-bleed hero / banner images                              |

Don't invent sizes (`w480`, `w900`, …); they aren't registered and will fall back to the original full-size file.

### Full-Bleed Images: `img-cover` + `img-cover-block`

`.img-cover` uses `position: absolute` to fill its parent. It must live inside an `.img-cover-block` wrapper (the wrapper provides the positioning context and the aspect ratio). Never put `.img-cover` on a small contained image — it'll absolutely-position to the nearest positioned ancestor and break the layout.

```php
<div class="img-cover-block">
	<?php
	echo wp_get_attachment_image(
		$image_id,
		'w1400',
		false,
		[ 'class' => 'img-cover', 'loading' => 'lazy', 'sizes' => '100vw' ]
	);
	?>
</div>
```

### Linked Images: `img-link`

When an attachment image is wrapped in an `<a>`, add the `img-link` class to the anchor so the project's link styles know to suppress the default underline / hover treatment around the image.

```php
<a href="<?php echo esc_url( $link['url'] ); ?>" class="img-link">
	<?php echo wp_get_attachment_image( $image_id, 'w768', false, [ 'class' => 'img-cover', 'loading' => 'lazy' ] ); ?>
</a>
```
